Should the raspberry pi need to be re-imaged/re-flashed after making the Python code file go through these steps.

1. Final assumptions (important)
-Script path: /home/arespi/fast_gpio.py
-videos saved to: /home/arespi/spoolvids/

2. Ensure directories & permissions
-Run once (in bash):
mkdir -p /home/arespi/spoolvids
chmod 755 /home/arespi/spoolvids

-> Make the script executable (no response is expected): chmod +x /home/arespi/fast_gpio.py

3. Create the systemd service
-create the service file (in bash):
sudo nano /etc/systemd/system/spoolcam.service

-once made paste exactly this:
[Unit]
Description=Spool Motion Recorder (MJPEG, GPIO-triggered)
After=multi-user.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=arespi
WorkingDirectory=/home/arespi
ExecStart=/usr/bin/python3 /home/arespi/fast_gpio.py
Restart=always
RestartSec=2
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target

-Once pasted hit: CTRL + O -> Write Out, ENTER -> confirm filename, CTRL + X -> exit editor

At this point, the service file exists, but systemd does not know about it yet.

4. Enable & start the service
-Run (in bash):
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable spoolcam.service
sudo systemctl start spoolcam.service

5. Verify it is running
-Run (in bash):
sudo systemctl status spoolcam.service

-> To stop manually run (in bash): sudo systemctl stop spoolcam.service this
-> This will stop the program for not, but will not interfere with the code from starting once it gets powered on again.

6.
-Reboot (in bash):
sudo reboot

-ssh back into the raspberry pi and either check the spoolvids file for new mjpeg files or run (in bash):
sudo systemctl status spoolcam.service

7. Safe shutdown note

-Always stop recording before power removal run (in bash): sudo systemctl stop spoolcam.service 
